From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan <ryan@esterlines.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 15:04:55 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] LibWeb: Give back the memory used to build the content
 blocker's engine

Each WebContent process builds the content blocker's engine on its main
thread, and building it allocates several times what the engine keeps:
the lists split and parsed, and the flatbuffer builder. Once that is
freed, mimalloc keeps the pages in the main thread's heap, which nothing
collects (ThreadPool only collects its own threads when they go idle),
so a WebContent process with uBlock Origin's default lists measured
about 65 MiB more than the engine itself for as long as it lived.

Collect the heap once the engine is built or dropped.
---
 Libraries/LibWeb/Loader/ContentBlocker.cpp | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Libraries/LibWeb/Loader/ContentBlocker.cpp b/Libraries/LibWeb/Loader/ContentBlocker.cpp
index c36546c8454159220c3cab865dd1d5639b74b3a3..004bc2a6667e8113ae1b3c798b31625cd8635696 100644
--- a/Libraries/LibWeb/Loader/ContentBlocker.cpp
+++ b/Libraries/LibWeb/Loader/ContentBlocker.cpp
@@ -160,6 +160,10 @@ ErrorOr<void> ContentBlocker::set_patterns(ReadonlySpan<String> patterns)
 
 ErrorOr<void> ContentBlocker::set_rules_from_bytes(ReadonlyBytes rules_bytes, ReadonlyBytes trusted_rules_bytes, ReadonlyBytes resources_bytes)
 {
+    // NB: Building an engine allocates several times what the engine keeps, on this thread, whose heap nothing else
+    //     collects. Give that memory back, and an old engine's, rather than keeping it for the life of the process.
+    ScopeGuard collect_memory = [] { ak_kmalloc_collect(); };
+
     // OPTIMIZATION: Building an engine costs the same tokenizing and flatbuffer work whether or not there are any
     //               rules to put in it, and every caller already treats a missing engine as "nothing is blocked".
     //               Drop the engine instead of rebuilding an empty one.
